The U.S. has a fine tradition of freedom of the press, established in
the Constitution and in many court decisions.  It is important to
extend this tradition as directly as possible to electronic media.
A link in a WWW page is like a reference in a book.  To refer to
another book is not a violation of its copyright nor does the person
making the reference take responsibility for the content of the work
referred to.  The lawyers should take responsibility for protecting
academic freedom and freedom of the press in electronic media.

Another legal issue is "fair use" of copyrighted material.  Electronic
publishers are claiming controls over use of their material that print
publishers have not been able to establish.  I believe that at one
time print publishers took a dim view of public libraries but were
beaten in court.

Have some lawyers writtten articles about these issues?
