Technically, it still does.  Admittedly, the Comic Book Code has lost a 
*lot* of its influence in the community and has been weakened a bit, but 
you *still* will not see any non-CCA approved comics on newsstands in the US.

Just *try* to find *any* of the Vertigo titles, or Grendal, or Batman: 
Legend of the Dark Knight, or *most* indies, or *any* comic without the 
CCA Seal of Approval for that instance on the newsstands here.  You 
*can't*...the stores and many newsstands will absolutely NOT carry any 
non-CCA-approved comics, and often are forbidden to do so by policy or, 
in rare cases, law.  In Britain, they sell (for example) 2000AD right on the 
newsstands...and I *promise* you much of the material in that comic 
would *never* get the Comic Code Seal of Approval, even with it being lax 
nowadays. :)

In any case, the comics community is only *now* recovering from the 
effect of the CCA, and keeps a careful eye out for any attempts at 
reinstatement.  (Right now there is a case being pursued involving the 
comic "American Flagg" (an adult title) being sold in a pack to a minor;
there is fear in the comics community that this case could well lead to a 
strengthening of the CCA, or even outright bans...the recent case 
involving the artist Mike C. Diana (whose indie comic "Boiled Angel" was 
ruled obscene and resulted in Diana being prohibited to draw for three 
years as a condition of his probation) does little to encourage hope.)

