OK...OK...

With respect to a suggestion that I read Mill's "On Liberty," I had 
previously written:

> I've read it.  I must have missed the part where he recommended 
> alt.binaries.giant.hooters for the elevation of the mind.

I've read all the replies, and I admit my statement was too sarcastic, 
too terse, and unworthy of the seriousness of the topic.  I believe, 
however, that my point was at least in part correct.  We are squandering 
our intellectual, artistic, and cultural heritage by glorifying the 
inability to make the simplist distinction between those things that are 
worthy and those that are not.  "Adult" art, of course, isn't.  It is 
comically juvenile.  The CMU library (I am sure) does not stock "Debbie 
Does Dallas" because there is no reason to.  Such works are perverted 
and degrading pieces of worthless trash.  If universities ASPIRE to 
produce graduates unable to distinguish art from trash, virtue from 
evil, the vulgar from the elevated, we are doomed.  Much of the debate 
on the "banning" of alt.sex.* had that ring to it.

I admit there are reasonable grounds to object to CMU's position.  So, 
if I still deserve it, flame away.

