The Internet School Networking Working Group has produced a draft entitled
"Ways to Define User Expectations <draft-ietf-isn-expectations-00.txt>"
which supercedes a prior draft entitled "Acceptable Use Policy Definition 
<draft-ietf-isn-aup-01.txt>".  It is available via the InterNIC gopher:

      URL: gopher://ds1.internic.net:70/00/internet-drafts
                              /draft-ietf-isn-expectations-00.txt

With the connection of high schools and even grade schools to the net,
there has been considerable discussion among school administrators 
about workable acceptable use policies.

The better ones which I have seen address issues such as tresspass (i.e.,
using others' passwords), theft (software piracy), privacy (e-mail), etc.
in just about the same terms as conventional school policy on 
non-cyberspace ethics and deportment.

And, as far as public expressions such as www pages or posts to usenet,
the rules are the same as those for student deportment in public places...
i.e., because the name of the school appears in the post or the address,
the student is should conduct himself/herself in such a manner as to
respectfully represent the school to the outside world.

While I can see some individuals pushing against these guidelines, for
the most part they are simple, straight forward, and useful.

Do take a look at the InterNIC draft cited above which is a product of the 
Internet School Networking Working Group of the Internet Engineering 
Task Force (IETF).




