I am a bit confused by this, are you saying that if the kids stopped then 
they have shitty parents?

In fact, the kids did stop themselves.  I do not disagree with your 
apparant premise about the importance of parental responsibility, but I 
do think that you may find that your perspective changes as you age and 
have children.

When I was younger and childless I believed very strongly in 
anti-censorship and free speech.  Now I find that while I am still 
pro-free speech that I am more interested in ways to make my community 
better than I am in the context-free aspects of free speech.

As an example, this whole Power Rangers episode, including the death of 
the 5 year old girl, has given me an opportunity to talk with my 6 year 
old, Molly, about these issues of responsibility, violence, and free speech.

Last night I told her about the Power Ranger murder.  One of her first 
responses was 'maybe they shouldn't even have Power Rangers.'  This 
opened the way to more conversations about being responsible and about 
free speech.  I was then able to tie free speech back into first grade 
terms about respecting other people when they are talking, and general 
politeness.  I feel that I was both true to my belief in free-speech, 
while at the same time helping to advance in her a better understanding 
of the consequences of violence.


