Again, this is your right and privilege.  I think that I am trying to 
work to a view of life that respects the rights of those who wish to do 
things that do not better the society in which our children, and the 
children who we love, must live, while at the same time supporting those 
efforts that make our society (wherever that may be) a better place.

There is an African proverb that is part of the philosophy of my 
daughter's school, that says 'it takes the whole village to raise a 
child.'  In our western societies it is everyones right to opt out of 
this philosophy, but it is clear to me that all children are better off 
when more people care about what is best for the kids.

The issue then comes up as to who defines what is 'best.'  As a 
practical matter I believe that is irrelevant.  Many of our neighbors are 
highly religous and have ideas about child raising that we flat 
disagree with.  In the day to day world of our daughter this makes no 
difference.  Our basic goal is to do what we think is best for our 
daughter, and at a secondary level, what is best for the children in our 
neighborhood.  Our neighbors have the same goal.  Doctrinal differences 
never even come up.

The question 'Do the Power Rangers make my community a better place?' is 
far removed from the censorious premise 'Should bad speech be prohibited.'

I believe that it is not censorship to recognize and work to counter the 
economic and cultural incentives that we have created to encourage 
basically harmful behavior (such as the Power Rangers).



