Carnegie Mellon's Student Senate (legislative branch of Student
Government) passed the following resolution tonight.  It will be
presented to university administrators at start-of-business tomorrow
morning.

Student Senate Resolution
F94-14
Tuesday, November 8, 1994

Whereas,

The administration's initial decision to censor electronic news groups
was made hastily, without obtaining an external expert legal opinion,
and without soliciting the opinions of the campus community, 

And whereas,

The University's current policy is reactionary and does not permit
student input with regard to the value of electronic news groups,

And whereas,

The student body is outraged at this act of censorship, and is of the
opinion that censorship in any form is intolerable,

And whereas,

The student body is concerned that this act may lead to future acts of
indiscriminate censorship,

And whereas,

The student body demands its University-guaranteed rights of academic freedom,

Be it resolved that,

A standing committee will be convened under the Office of the President
of the University by Friday, November 11, 1994, in order to address
issues pertaining to computer based communications.

The committee will consist of students, faculty members, and staff
members, each of which will be chosen by their respective governing
bodies.

The purpose of the committee will be to ensure that each of the
aforementioned groups is duly represented in any decision by the
administration that may impinge on an individual's right to the freedom
of speech, and/or his/her access to electronic news groups which are not
deemed to be in violation of applicable local, state, and federal laws.

Be it further resolved that,

The administration will review the decision to eliminate electronic
newsgroups from bboard services and, with the advisement of both the
aforementioned committee and a legal expert, will come to an equitable
solution which will adhere to the applicable local, state and federal
laws while maintaining maximum access to electronic newsgroups.

----

Burt Holzman
Chair, Student Senate





