Subject: The CERT/CMU login-banner abomination
Date: 9 Nov 1994 01:15:41 -0500
Message-ID: <39pped$a7a@eff.org>

While you folks at CMU are at it, please try to stop the CERT/CMU
"policy virus". It has already spread to a dozen universities.

The proposed banning of alt.sex isn't the only CMU policy based
(apparently) on an incompetent understanding of the law.  In 1992, the
CERT organization, based at CMU, gave legal advice that I believe
potentially strips computer users at CMU and elsewhere of all privacy
rights.

Here is the current login banner at unix1.andrew.cmu.edu:

====================
 This system is for the use of authorized users only. 
Unauthorized use may be monitored and recorded. 
In the course of such monitoring or through system 
maintenance, the activities of authorized users 
may be monitored.

 By using this system you expressly consent to such 
monitoring.

=====================

Here the CERT's original suggestion:

       This system is for the use of authorized users only.
       Individuals using this computer system without authority, or in
       excess of their authority, are subject to having all of their
       activities on this system monitored and recorded by system
       personnel.

       In the course of monitoring individuals improperly using this
       system, or in the course of system maintenance, the activities
       of authorized users may also be monitored.

CERT's suggested text is awful. It says (essentially) that if a school
thinks it might ever need to capture the keystrokes of an intruder,
then should compel its users to give up their ECPA and (at state
universities) 4th Amendment rights.

Note the banner does not restrict itself to keystroke monitoring. It
does not defining "monitoring". Rather, it says that all activities
might be monitored and recorded. This could include email. It could
include anything. It does not restrict itself to intruders or to break
in attempts. Rather, it says that even authorized users in excess of
their authority are subject to being "monitored" and "recorded". Since
users don't have authority to violate rules, this all means that any
user suspected of rule breaking loses his or her right email (and
other) privacy.

Some of the enclosed references, have suggestions for narrower, tighter
wording. I'm not even sure a banner is really necessary.

- Carl

