A few random thoughts of my own on this Power Rangers debate:

1) Most parents I have heard argue against government interferance have 
   also commented that they have decided that the show is too violent for
   *their* children and do not let them watch it.  If they are already
   censoring their children's view of the world, then why be concerned
   about the government also having that power?  If they were people
   saying they *encouraged* their children to watch it, I would feel more
   sympathetic.

2) Every time I read someone say something along the lines of "I used
   to be strongly against censorship, but now ..." I feel I should 
   point out that almost always people become more in favour of 
   censorship as they age, since their views and the views of their 
   generation are now heard and accepted more widely.  There is a
   wisp of hypocracy in always arguing what will advance one's own
   ideas.

3) Not displaying the consequences of violence: I think there would be
   a major uproar if the Power Rangers or any other children's program
   showed the corspes of civilians caught in the crossfire, or the 
   gore of being hit with real bullets as opposed to an ambiguous 
   energy bolt. 

4) To follow up on 3: if a cartoon wants to show gore, it uses robots
   or magical automatons that look and act like people, but are 
   "morally acceptable" to shoot at will. (A years or two ago, I saw a
   show - Batman I think - where a villian was dropped several stories
   into an active spotlight.  There was then a close-up on the debris
   to prove that he was, in fact, just a robot).

5) Violence on the schoolyard has always existed (my mother's stories
   of what she used to do are a lot more violent than anything I ever
   did!).  Now it bends to follow the current fad program (which is
   designed to tap children at an emotional level).  Just because you
   can recognize a pattern in bahaviour does not mean that the show
   *caused* the violence, just the form the violence takes.

6) With about 300 million people living in North America, there are
   going to always be a few strange and unfortunate deaths of children
   playing. It is easy to condem something because a death seems to be
   linked to it (I say "seems" only because I have become more cynical
   of the connections people make after looking closely at the anti- 
   role-playing hysteria of a few years ago).

  I personally haven't made up my mind which side (if any) of this issue 
I agree with (although I do lean towards free speech).  There are 
powerful arguments either way and I have seen no single solution that makes
me comfortable.  The medium itself may become obsolete before the battle 
over its content is finished.

Thomas Cook.


