At McGill University in Montreal, the Academic Senate will vote today
to approve a modified Student Code.  The modifications stem from
past incidences in which university officials searched through student
e-mail and presented private and personal correspondence as evidence
against students in disciplinary proceedings.  Expensive lawyers
got involved in the prolonged hearings, and even the Dean of Students
says "we made a total mess of it", when recalling the bungled affair.
 
The solution, to some it seems, is to prevent students from hiring lawyers
and change the student code to strip students of a right they currently
enjoy: electronic privacy.  As it stands, article #1 of the McGill
student code invokes the Canadian and Quebec Charters of Rights and
Freedoms, -- the latter including an explicit right to privacy.
One McGill department, Electrical Engineering, even has an
explicit departmental policy affirming the right to privacy
for electronic communications.
 
All this is set to change today.
 
As McGill Law Professor Julius Grey, a well known constutional law expert, says
 
  "We may as well say that democracy ends at the [McGill's] Roddick gates"
 
(quote from Montreal HOUR, April 14, 1994 vol 2, no 11, page 7)


