media.  When government tries to tell me what I can or cannot say, look 
at or read, I'll go down fighting.  No, I don't generally spend my time 
looking at the type of material under discussion, but if I choose to, I 
expect to have that right, and I'll fight for anyone else's right to do 
the same.  We now face censorship at its worst:  educators are prohibited 
from referring to God in the classroom.  If the battle against censorship 
is not fought with a vengence on all fronts, what will be next?  Will we 
eliminate all of the sit-coms on TV?  In case you haven't noticed, they 
are rampant with violence and sexual inuendos.  If we do that, then next 
we'll have to ban radio and TV broadcasts of religious services because 
they MIGHT offend someone.  Next, we'll have to require that merchants 
stop displaying and selling religious symbols at Christmas.

The list goes on and on.

As with all of the rights guaranteed under the constitution, everyone is 
entitled to these rights.  And, my rights end ONLY at the point at which 
they infringe upon the rights of others.  

Please explain to me how my choice of reading material in any way 
infringes upon your rights.

For reference to violence and crime, see above.

As for the matter of children accessing "inappropriate" material:  
Perhaps an arguement can be made to support the claim that it is "harmful 
to them;"  but it comes back to one assuming responsibility for one's own 
actions.  First, the issue of the availability of internet in the public 
schools--If CMU has the ability to restrict access to certain material, 
obviously, the public schools have that same ability in which setting, I 
believe it could be justified.
Second, if the concern is access from home--I suppose someone might want 
to point out that this remains within the domain of the parent (most 
homes still have at least ONE).
Third, if the little darlings want to get at the information, GUESS WHAT??
They'll find a way, and no amount of censorship or bells and whistles is 
going to prevent it.  Case in point:  Where in the U.S. is it legal for a 
minor to possess a weapon?--Surprise, more than a few children have them.
Where in the U.S. is it legal for them to buy tobacco products?--Again, 
they have it, and the surprising thing in this case is that in most 
states, there is no law prohibiting its use by minors.

Do we need to make every reasonable effort to keep this matterial out of 
the hands of children?  Yes, of course.

The question is how do you define reasonable.  Most of us believe that it 
is spelled Personal and parental responsibility.

