Get a clue.  Your proposition works fine for well-off families who can
afford to have one parent in the home all the time monitoring every activity 
of the kids (and not, of course, allowing them to go anywhere else in the 
world where there might be a TV--which is EVERYWHERE--I've even picked my kid 
up a few minutes late from a swim class at the rec center to find him perched 
with dozens of others in front of the big screen tv in the lobby). Such 
well-off parents might also opt to pay a fortune to send their kids to  
carefully regulated child-care programs in which no tv is ever shown.  But 
both options--one parent at home constantly and the kids never going out, or 
the kids warehoused in a carefully controlled program--take MONEY.  The rest 
of us have to live with our kids in a world where TV is simply a part of 
background noise.

I don't support censorship.  Though I rather intensely dislike Power Rangers 
for a variety of reasons, I don't think it's right that they be banned from 
the airways.  I agree that parents have to accept primary responsibility for 
their kids' tv viewing, along with a lot else.  And I even agree that kids 
should not dictate their activities to their parents.  But I think there would 
have to be something desperately wrong in a household where children NEVER saw 
tv, because it would mean that the parents were dictating their kids 
activities one hundred per cent of the time (if you do that, when do kids 
start to learn how to make good choices for themselves?  suddenly, at age 18 
or 21, they just know how?) AND it would mean that the kids never went 
anywhere.  

Just out of idle curiousity, are you a parent?  If so, have you actually 
managed to raise children who have never seen tv?  If so, how?  Got 'em locked 
in a closet somewhere?  If not, why not?  Are you a "parent who can't parent?"

I'll put my money on your not being a parent at all.

