During my father's lifetime, we had no television in the house.

Of course, my friends had TV, but I didn't spend ALL my time there,
or even a large part of it.  At home, there was talking with family,
reading books, listening to records (almost all classical), and
tinkering with hobby-projects -- all of these were encouraged.  So was
listening to the adult/academic conversation when my parents' colleagues
came to visit; it wasn't thought "harmful" to hear discussions of topics
far advanced beyond a child's understanding, but rather the reverse.

My father, by the way, was a professor at Purdue University; the honors
award in biology is named after him.  I think he knew EXACTLY what the
effect would be of not having TV, and all those other little decisions.

I don't think I missed much.  I had a good solid grounding, not only in
the sciences (to be expected from two scientist parents), but in a wide
range of literature.  And I was VERY politically aware and active -- at
age 15, cranking the mimeograph and distributing literature for the
Humphrey '68 campaign (my father was dead by then, but he'd pegged
Nixon as a liar back in '60); becoming the youngest member ever in my
state's Civil Liberties Union; writing to papers and getting published.
At 16 I joined Mensa (to quit years later over policy issues), and then
spent three successive summers at university camps for advanced study.

My "age-group peers" were still spending their spare time watching TV.

I think you could do worse for your kids than get rid of the boob tube.
Just make sure they have something else -- like books, hobbies, and
their parents -- to spend time with.  Honest, it won't hurt their minds.

