In article <9NOV199421480872@vax2.concordia.ca>,
DAVID KURZMAN <davek@vax2.concordia.ca> wrote:
>What the hello is a PGP signature?

   PGP (Pretty Good Privacy) is a public key encryption program.  One
function of PGP is to hash a plaintext file.  The result is a signature.

   Okay, in English...  :-)

   A plaintext file is a simple text file (but PGP can also use a binary
file).  A hash function (sometimes called a message digest) is a function
that uses a plaintext file to generate a number (called the signature). 
Ideally, the hash function should generate a unique signature for any given
plaintext file.  The reason why the PGP signature looks like garbled text is
because the number has been converted to printable ascii text.  The
signature is usually quite a big number (I forget the actual size that PGP
uses, but judging by the signature, it must be about 64 or 128 bytes--that's
a number in the range of 0 and 2^(64*8) or 2^(128*8)--very big!)  :-)

   Why bother with all this?  It is easy to check whether a message has been
altered by running it through the message digest and comparing signatures. 



