At the Senate meeting on Wednesday, SECC (Student Electronic Communications
Collective) distributed a one page opinion on the proposed changes to the
student code, especially from a computer perspective. Here is the full 
text of the opinion. What was distributed - though - was not all of this, 
but only parts of it to make it shorter.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Subject:      Senate Statement
X-To:         SECC <secc-l@vm1.mcgill.ca>
To: Multiple recipients of list SECC-L <SECC-L@VM1.MCGILL.CA>


The Student Electronic Communications Collective of McGill

The Student Electronic Communications Collective (SECC) is a
group of students who are concerned with some of the proposed
ammendments to the "Code of Student Conduct and Disciplinary
Procedures." Many of the proposed changes violate the Quebec and
Canadian Charter of Rights which a student is entitled to under
section 1 of "The Charter of Students' Rights."

* Sections 15(b) and 15(c) violate a student's right to be
presumed innocent until proven guilty. Such a stance in a
hearing has already placed the Hearing Committee in a biased
position.

* Section 40 actually forgoes the civil and criminal laws
guranteed in section 1 of the Charter of Students' Rights in the
definition of the admissability of evidence.

* Section 12(b) suspends a student's rights to privacy based on
an accusation; where the evidence to justify the accusation can
be obtained by any means necessary through section 40.

There is also a redundacy in section 12(b) since many of the
procedural duties that may compromise a student's right to
privacy are covered in the "Code of Conduct for Users of McGill
Computing Facilities."

================================================================
1 Every student enjoys within the University all rights and
freedoms recognized by law.

22 Every student is presumed innocent of a disciplinary offence
unless he or she is found guilty on the basis of clear,
convincing and reliable evidence laid against him or her.

12b) No student shall knowingly use any authorization or
knowingly misuse passwords, codewords or similar means of access
to computers, or knowingly use the facilities in a manner which
would interfere with the access or integrity of the accounts of
other users, or knowingly violate the "Code of Conduct for Users
of McGill Computing Facilities." A student so accused may not
claim that communications made through the University computer
system are confidential, notwithstanding any implications of
Article 40, so long as the communications in question were
obtained only by authorized University employees acting upon
reasonable grounds to protect the integrity of the system in
accordance with the "Code of Conduct for Users of McGill
Computing Facilities."

15b) Upon demonstrating that the student has represented and
submitted another person's work as his or her own, it shall be
presumed that the student intended to deceive; the student shall
bear the burden of rebutting this presumption by evidence
satisfying the person or body hearing the case that no such
intent existed, notwithstanding Article 22 of the Charter of
Students' Rights.

15c) No student shall, with intent to deceive, contribute any
work to another student with the knowledge that the latter may
submit the work in part or whole as their own. Receipt of
payment for work contributed shall be cause for  presumption
that the student intended to deceive (notwithstanding Article
22 of the Charter of Students' Rights); the Student shall bear
the burden of rebutting this presumption by evidence satisfying
the person or body hearing the case that no such intent existed.

40 The rules of evidence applicable in civil and criminal court
proceedings shall not apply to the hearing. Evidence which is not
relevant shall be excluded. All questions of the admissibility
of evidence shall be resolved by the Chair of the Committee after
consulting with the legal assessor, as per article 36a).

55f) Only if the Dean has reasonable grouds to believe that the
student's continued presence on campus poses an immediate threat
to the safety or academic integrity of the community, he or she
may suspend the student for a period not exceeding 30 days
(examinations missed or papers not submitted because of
suspension shall be counted as unexcused absences or lateness,
subject to Article 57 b) herein).



