Date: 11 Nov 1994 05:41:41 GMT
Message-ID: <39v06l$quh@sifon.cc.mcgill.ca>

The Student Electronic Communications Collective of McGill - let's call it
SECC for brevity - has decided to take a further stance to defend student
rights at McGill. 

Our action plan is below...but first...

We have noted that student's rights are infringed (or possibly infringed) 
in three main areas, not limited strictly to computer areas;

      a) That students' fundamental freedom to privacy (as per section
	eight of the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedom) is being
	violated by the proposed addition to section 12(b) of the 
	Charter of Student Rights whereby a student accused of 
	interfering with computer systems "may not claim that 
	communications made through the University computer system
	are confidential..." This is direct violation of the right
	to privacy of email accounts. It is our interpretation that
	the Supreme Court of Canada holds a similar view on privacy
	of communications, based on R. v Plant (1993). 

    b) That section 22 of the Student Code will violate a basic tenant 
	of our legal system: the right to be presumed innocent.

   	That section 15 of the Student Code will also violate the 
	students' right to be presumed innocent until proved guilty.

    d) That section 40 of the Student Code will be tantamount to
	allowing all illegally obtained evidence into hearings, 
	contradicting current legal procedure. 

These points of objection were not necessarily original to SECC and
have not been covered in detail above. 

Students have less than two weeks to defend their rights. In two
weeks the proposed changes to the student codes will go before
Senate. It is our feeling that the Senate will not postpone a
vote on the changes another time. If the proposed changes go 
through unchanged, as law professor Julias Grey said, "we might
as well say that democracy ends at the [McGill's] Roddick Gates."

SECC is preparing a number of Information Packages about the matter. 
These will include history, issues, objections, alternate proposals,
official opinions and legal opinions on whether the Senates changes are 
Constitutional. 

Packages will be given to the public media, student media, and 
V.I.P.s at McGill. There is a possibility that SECC will work
with SSMU in preparing the packages.  

It is hoped that with enough pressure the Senate will rethink its'
proposal in order to undergo further consultation with students
and that the Senate will modify its' proposal to make it less
totalitarian, less Orwellian. 

It is SECC's belief - however - that the proposal did not 
necessarily have "bad intentions" behind it, but that the wording 
nonetheless undermines the rights that students enjoy under
Candian and Quebec law, and sound less than reassuring.

McGill has a had history of "not being nice" to students' rights, 
and also to fundamental rights with respects to electronic 
communications.

For more details on SECC's plans, contact 

