>My vague recollection is that in the first Dial-A-Porn case, the
>Supreme Court said that Dial-A-Porn could not be banned "to protect
>children" (even through telephones are heavily regulated). Does anyone
>know the case? According to one of my books it was decided in June
>1989, but I don't know the name.

The case you're looking for is Sable Communications, Inc v. FCC, 492 U.S. 
115, 16 Med. L. Rptr. 1961 (1989).  The Court actually held that 
_indecent_  (i.e., explicit but not Miller-test obscene) messages could 
not be banned.  The Court upheld the statute in question as to obscene 
materials (which probably presents the same interesting local standards 
problems as last summer's Memphis trial of a California BBS provider).  
Further, indecent messages can still be regulated/restricted, as long as 
the government can prove it has chosen the "least restrictive means" 
available to further the articulated "compelling interest." I think the 
gov't went back to the drawing board and now requires providers of 
sexually explicit phone services to require a credit card (or some such 
thing, in an attempt to restrict access by minors).

