     The problem with the CMU banner is that it is not some kind
of admonition about authorization and  security  but rather is a
general waiver of all privacy rights that one must accept by
implication each time one logs onto the system.
 
     What if some law enforcement agency though that perhaps
someone associated with CMU might be doing something illegal or
at least recieving e-mail from someone who was (probably a sure
bet).  Could that law enforcement agency just issure a blanket
subpeona for everything in the system or would they need to take
the much more difficult steps needed to get a specific (and
court-ordered) search warrent or wire tap authorization?
 
     I would hope that if any system operator got a broad
subpeona for its users' files and communications it would
challenge it in court and assert that that information was only
available via a narrowly drawn and specific warrent issured by a
proper court for good cause. (I'll bet the ACLU would help if
necessary.)
 
     But in such a challenge to a subpeona a central question
for the judge would be whether on not the users of the system in
question had a "reasonable expectation of privacy."  The fact
that all the users had for years agreed to waive all privacy
rights each time they logged onto the system could be used
to claim that the users did not have the expectation of privacy
that would make a search warrent necessary.
 
     In the ACLU letter about the CMU ban on alt.sex.*
newsgroups it is said that the decision and its explanation had
been issued by CMU without consulting its counsel.  I wonder if
CMU could have consulted its counsel about the waiver of privacy
being inserted in its logon bannar.  I don't see how setting the
school up to be an easy target for some DA who wants to make a
name for himself by exposing "smutt" or some such thing could be
