                             PETER BERGER'S SPEECH
                                       
   
     _________________________________________________________________
   
   
   
   ``Hi. My name is Pete Berger. I'm a lawyer for Telerama Internet here
   in Pittsburgh, and I'm also a CMU alumnus.
   
   Other speakers today are explaining why CMU's decision is not based on
   any reading of the law that doesn't come from a fantasy novel, so I
   won't linger on that. I'm here to talk about the big picture. I want
   to talk about what this means for CMU faculty, CMU students, and the
   reputation of my alma mater.
   
   I have a T-shirt that I got here a long time ago. I know a lot of you
   own that T-shirt also. It's black, it's got the CMU logo on it, and it
   says `MIT: The CMU of Massachussetts.'
   
   I don't think I can wear that T-shirt anymore.
   
   There are, when you get right down to it, two types of universities.
   World class ones like MIT, Harvard, Yale, and Columbia -- and everyone
   else. World class institutions understand their role as the protectors
   of free expression. They do not shy away from taking an unpopular
   stance because it is morally and ethically right to do so. CMU is
   taking the moral low road. This horribly embarrassing decision to
   restrict academic freedom is a shout to the world that CMU has grown
   tired of its reputation as a world class institution, and would prefer
   the quiet but perhaps lucrative role of a third-rate academic
   backwater.
   
   The materials CMU is trying to get rid of were used by a CMU grad
   student, so I understand, to write his thesis. One can only wonder
   what other academic resources will be squandered in this incredible
   quest for mediocrity. Faculty members: how will your peers react to
   you when they know that you are a member of an institution that is
   afraid to stand up for the right most essential to academia?
   
   I had a conversation recently with an administrator who was speaking
   in favor of the university's decision. I was, frankly, giving him a
   pretty hard time about it. He looked at me and said `You don't
   understand. I hate this. I hate being on this side of the argument.'
   Well, I do understand. And that's why I'd like to extend an invitation
   to all of you who have supported this decision because of the legal
   misinformation you were given to come on over to the correct side of
   the argument.
   
   There are some simple truths in this world. You can't be just a little
   bit dead. You can't be just a little bit pregnant. And you can't throw
   away just a little bit of your academic integrity and expect the world
   to respect you for it.
   
   I urge the faculty and administration of this institution with such a
   fine tradition of leadership to make the right decision and erase this
   degrading chapter in your history by reversing the decision to censor.
   Reverse this decision to become a third-rate intellectual backwater.
   Change Carnegie-Mellon into something that students, faculty, staff,
   administration, and alumni can be proud of again -- instead of
   something we are embarrassed of.
   
   Thank you.




