                             DONNA RILEY'S SPEECH
                                       
   
     _________________________________________________________________
   
   
   
   Yesterday it was brought to my attention that an anti-pornography
   group commended CMU for protecting its female students from harmful
   images and texts on the internet.
   
   I can't deny that some of the material I've encountered on these
   bboards reflects and perpetuates violence against women. But
   censorship of information is NOT the answer. In fact, CMU is doing its
   women a great disservice.
   
   We're big girls, President Mehrabian, give us some credit. We've seen
   this stuff before - in fact, it permeates our culture. But the
   internet is one of the few places where we can fight back on a more
   even footing, using the most powerful tools we have - our
   imaginations, our wits, and our keyboards. President Mehrabian, I
   implore you: get out of our way!!!
   
   CMU women don't need the protection of condescending administrators,
   lawyers, and statesmen in this matter. The words and images on the
   bboards CMU censored are only one of many conduits for sexism in our
   culture. We need the space and autonomy to fight the attitudes that
   hurt us - in our own ways, and on our own ground.
   
   CMU women don't need to be sheltered from a bunch of horny men's dirty
   pictures and stories on the net - we need to understand and contribute
   to the ongoing dialogue about sexuality and consent, about power and
   gender.
   
   CMU women don't need this attempt to control certain sexual
   expressions in cyberspace. In a culture that places so many
   restrictions on female sexuality, we need to be empowered and
   encouraged to express our desires in whatever way we damn well please.
   
   
   We must be free to say YES to sex and demand it on our own terms. We
   must build a movement that is ever curious about sex - one which
   reclaims an eroticism not defined by racist, sexist, or heterosexist
   perspectives, one which thrives on diversity and openness. The
   internet is one environment in which such an eroticism can grow,
   through both critical debate and creative brainstorming.
   
   The feminist movement seeks to reduce the danger women face in sex
   while broadening the possibilities for pleasure. A thoughtless
   expansion of all sexual options without a critique of the sexist
   structure in which they evolve only exposes women to more danger. On
   the other hand, an exclusive focus on danger and violence negates
   women's experiences of pleasure and fails to empower women with
   curiosity and sexual energy. We seek to resist not only sexual
   violence against women, but also sexual stigma, respression, and
   censorship.
   
   As Amber Hollibaugh writes, "Feminism must be an angry, uncompromising
   movement that is just as insistent about our right to fuck, our right
   to the beauty of our individual female desires, as it is concerned
   with the images and structures that distort it."
   
   Pro-sex feminists are, above all, committed to keeping the dialogue
   about sex open. We will never expand women's opportunities if we
   censor the material of this debate before we have begun.


