In article <3a0gpm$c01@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:

>[Here is Mike Godwin's speech from CMU's Freedom of Cyberspace rally.]

>[CMU has] forgotten that the Constitution
>presumptively protects speech...
>They've forgotten that, when it comes to the Bill
>of Rights, what you don't use, you lose.
>The First Amendment is a terrible thing to waste.

Those are good words Mike, but what was your point in
saying them when you knew you also had to say:

>...CMU, as a private institution, is not bound
>by the First Amendment, and therefore can do anything it
>likes. This is, of course, quite true...

You are usually among the first to correct misunderstanding
about what the First Amendment is and who it regulates.  Its
spirit is always worthy of support, but there is no "use it
or lose it" argument to be made here.  The right of private
institutions to regulate their own houses is not absolute;
neither is that of free speech.  Both, however, are worthy
of a respect that should begin with knowing which is which.

