>lex@interport.net (Stevens R. Miller), addressing Mike Godwin, writes:

>[...]
>>You are usually among the first to correct misunderstanding
>>about what the First Amendment is and who it regulates.
>[...]

>I think Mike's point was 1) the University originally claimed that the
>ban was a legal requirement 2) that the First Amendment precludes any
>such legal requirement.

Mike is more direct than that, but if that was his point I would dispute
it on two grounds: test cases do address untried law and, although the
default assumption is that speech is protected, defaults do not control,
and placing what could be deemed obscene material where minors can get it
would not necessarily be defensible on First Amendment grounds (and we
all know, too well, that what is available in the alt.* subtree can be
deemed obscene by the current version of the community standards doctrine).
Likewise, (and this, I submit, is also indisputable) child porn is also
available in alt.*.  Forgive me for trotting out that tired issue, but it
just won't go away as long as Denmark (or wherever it comes from) is on
the net.  The First Amendment wouldn't protect anyone in that area either.

Moreover, my concern is not with the interpretation you offer of Mike's
speech (it's viable, I concede), but the more likely confusion the lay
listener might fall into regarding what CMU's obligations are under the
law.  Yes, Mike does indeed say that CMU can do what it wants to and that
the issue is that it should want to do what Mike says it ought to want to,
even though the law does not compel it to.  Nevertheless, to say that all
private agencies must be ready to defend their right to First Amendment
protection by carrying alt.* or else be deemed abusers of free speech
seems to invert altogether the freedoms the Bill of Rights protects.  CMU
has no duty to defend the First Amendment.  Suggesting that their failure
to do so violates some affirmative obligation (the "should" in Mike's
speech) conflates the duties of government and the freedoms of the
governed.  To say that CMU endangers the First Amendment by giving into
its fears of prosecution (when, apparently, it ought to be braver than
that) may be logically consistent, but the speech obscures that logic by
first asserting the "use it or lose it" view of rights, only later to
explain who must do the using and against whom the rights must be enforced.
I strongly suspect that empassioned listeners may have felt that Mike's
call to arms was directed at them (rather than CMU) and that the rights
being violated were theirs (rather than CMU's).

