/ To: Multiple recipients of list <pac-hiway@cunews.carleton.ca>
/ Subject: Re: Should Universities intrude on e-mail

     In response to various comments and contributions about the proposed
McGill regulations, I would like to note that at Trent University in the
mid-1980s, following a student suicide at the local police station,
students were purported to have been involved in a theft of arms and other
military materials.  The police immediately requested that any of the
students' material on Trent computers be handed over to them. 
     At the time I was President of the University and after consultation
with senior academic and administrative staff and lawyers followed my
original instinct and requested that the police serve the University with
a search warrant if they wished to obtain the files, since I believed that
it would establish a precedent that the students' files were not their own
property.  It seemed to me that we ought not to take a student's
notebooks, drafts of letters or papers and the like and turn them over to
anyone, since we could not reasonably consider them University property.  
     The police served an appropriate warrant to which we complied and
this established for me what ought to be the procedure with respect to e-
mail, etc.  (Unfortunately, if we had not downloaded and locked the material
in the safe, it would have been turned over to the police without warrant by
some of the staff.) As we are gradually turning from a situation where
personal mail is more and more being processed electronically and not
sent by snail mail, should not the sanctity of any form of personal mail be
respected?  Should an employer be justified in opening  personal mail
received in electronic media rather than on paper and in an envelope? 
     It does not surprise me that the CCLA, which has never demonstrated
any deep interest in university issues, should avoid this issue; but if
they have genuinely taken the view that anything as sophisticated as a
microphone is beyond their mandate, their Ludditism will certainly make them
ineffective with respect to the broadest sense of civil liberties in our
new electronic technoculture.  I suspect (or perhaps hope) there is some
misunderstanding, since I have always had great admiration for Alan
Borovoy and have supported the CCLA. 
     If the McGill Senate actually passes such regulations, I think that
the Canadian EFF and other interested groups can depend on many members of
the academic community becoming actively involved.  It certainly should be
of interest as to whether the currently proposed legislation originally
emanated from the previous senior administration of McGill, since the
former Principal is now a key player in the examination of the
future of the Infohighway and its regulation in Canada, as Chair of the
committee, exploring the question for the federal government. 
     We must insist on the privacy of e-mail and personal files for 
everyone.  Besides it is crucial to the well-being of the research and 
teaching missions of the universities to protect this principle of 
privacy, since it is necessary to providing a secure and encouraging 
atmosphere for developing innovative research and for encouraging  
openness and originality in learning.  

				Donald Theall  


