Date: 12 Nov 1994 18:13:31 GMT
Message-ID: <3a30kb$8ur@casaba.srv.cs.cmu.edu>

I am a faculty member in the CMU Computer Science Department,  I'm speaking
only for myself here, not for CS or the university.

After appearing on WRCT on Friday night, and trying to make some sense of
the Administration's position since no one from Warner Hall was able to
attend (it was pretty short notice), I think I finally understand the real
CMU policy:

  Members of the campus community shall not use CMU computer facilities 
  to do anything that the Administration will have to take heat for.

Now it all becomes clear, and even makes sense:

* No controversial bboards on the Andrew system, because people might
  accuse the CMU Administration of being purveyors of porn, and
  WE DON'T NEED THE HASSLE.

I guess you can't blame them for fearing the press; look at the shoddy
coverage we've seen in the Post-Gazette this week.  Anyone heard from the
National Enquirer yet?

* CMU students are still allowed to FTP erotic images from other sites.  Why?
  Because this poses no risk of embarassing the administration.  WE CAN'T 
  BE HELD ACCOUNTABLE FOR WHAT THOSE WACKY STUDENTS DO ON THEIR OWN MACHINES.
  [throw hands in air]

Censoring selected FTP connections is technically difficult at present.  So
for now, Warner Hall is off the hook.

* Students may NOT set up a news server on a student-owned machine to make
  censored bboards available over the campus network, because CMU owns
  the campus network and could, speaking technically and not morally, shut
  down the server.  WE COULD BE BLAMED FOR FAILING TO TAKE EASY STEPS TO
  BLOCK YOUR TRANSMISSION OF THIS DATA, SO WE MUST BLOCK IT.

See?  It makes sense.  Note that the operative word is "blamed", not
"prosecuted".  Bad publicity, especially at the national level, robs the
university of a lot of the Administration's time and money, which really ARE
valuable things.  All these guys were trying to do was save us some bucks.

The Administration has stressed repeatedly that it doesn't wish to prevent
CMU students from accessing banned materials.  It just doesn't want to do
anything that could be seen as helping.  High level officials including the
President have said on multiple occasions that students are free to call up
Compuserve and download anything they want.  When I pointed out that a
student would be using a CMU-owned telephone to do that, my comment was
dismissed with the words "Well, we can't do anything about that." And
furthermore, they don't want to.  Here's why:

* Students can access whatever they want by phone because nobody will hold
  CMU responsible.  IF WE CAN'T BE BLAMED, THEN GO FOR IT DUDES!

I'm sure I don't need to point out that this policy has a some major weak
points.  Unwillingness to stand up to a gutter press and assorted political
rabble in defense of freedom of expression would be embarassing even to a
community college, much less a national university.  Appearing publicity-shy
is itself bad publicity, so it's been necessary to conjure up bogus legal
concerns to justify these otherwise indefensible actions.

Second, the policy underestimates the ability of the press to make trouble.
Even with the ban now in place, as soon as some reporter gets wind of how
students are circumventing it (legally, according to CMU's policy), we will
see headlines like: "Students Now Using CMU's Advanced Computer Network to
Access Porn Sites Around the World".  Anyone want to call the Enquirer
with this scoop?

The biggest problem is the fact that the policy is illogical.  CMU allows
and even encourages its students to communicate over the campus network.
And the new policy says students are still free to retrieve any images they
want by accessing off-campus sites.  But if the Computer Club exercises its
right to FTP files from banned newsgroups and then, via its student-owned
news server, allows people on campus to access this material, the
Administration will protest.  Why?  Because some bozo could still try to
hold CMU responsible in some way.  "We own the network, so we might get
blamed."

Forget about the common carrier argument; no reporter salivating over the
thought of a juicy scandal piece is going to listen to reason.  So we see
Warner Hall pressured into banning particular sorts of electronic
communication while still trying with good intentions to allow students to
do anything that they (the Administration) can't be held accountable for.
Unfortunately, as I've just shown, this makes the policy absurd.

I invite the Administration to return to the previous policy of no
censorship, as recommended by the Faculty Senate.  At the same time, they
should announce a new initiative to examine the many complex issues
associated with cyberspace, including copyright protection and the problems
of content control for young children.  Let's apply for grants, study all
the issues carefully, and hold a symposium to discuss the lessons we've
learned here.  That will be a lot more fun than trying to explain to a
stunned faculty why three newsgroups containing only cartoons (!) were
among the 15 that were banned this week.

  -- Dave Touretzky, Senior Research Scientist
     Computer Science Department
     CMU

