"Ian McEachern (im@hydra.carleton.ca) wrote:
": The TV airwaves are public property and a very scarce resource ... I, and
": the rest of the Canadian public have a right to demand that the resouce
": be used appropriately! The airwaves (including bandwidth on cable)
": should not be cluttered with programming which has so few, if any,
": redeaming values! 

By what moral rationale do you claim that the airwaves are "public
property", and further by what rationale do you claim that they should
be ruled by government.

Consider that the original development of radio technology was not
done under government contract, and for the most part expansion of
the currently-usable area of bandwidth (among other approaches for
effectively increasing the total capacity) were not done under
government contract.  Rather, these things were done by *individuals*
and *companies* working for their own expectation of profit.

Consider that the government does not provide broadcasting equipment,
receiving equipment, or radio bandwidth; it only provides -- for a fee --
that which it had no part in creating and effectively no part in making
usable, and does *not* reimburse, in any way, those who *do* make
it usable (save by selling them, for a fee, permission to use their
own creation, provided of course that the creators wish to use it 
within that country; the work of foreign creators is seized without
even this ludicrous compensation.)

Now try this -- the major obstacle to a massive increase in the total
usage of *currently* *existing* bandwidth, is government regulation.
The portion of the spectrum dedicated to each TV channel is what
was technologically necessary 40 or so years ago... today we could 
put a minimum of two channels (I'm tempted to say 4, as I think we
could easily compress the bandwidth required; instead I am considering
*only* the effect of multiplexing the signal, since one channel only
uses the video band 50% of the time) in the same space, and have room 
left over, *but* it would be *illegal* for anyone to actually broadcast 
in this fashion in that frequency range.

Bandwidth is a government-controlled resource because goverment
has by armed force seized control of it and promises to use armed
force to enforce its seizure.  Bandwidth is a scarce resource
because government requires it to be scarce and won't permit
efficient use of it -- a situation possibly only because it
is a government-controlled resource.

